00:06:42.996 --> 00:06:44.669
it's that, oh,

00:06:44.669 --> 00:06:46.241
and it's also, you know, that happened

00:06:46.241 --> 00:06:47.620
to a brother-in-law of mine,

00:06:47.620 --> 00:06:50.069
or my grandfather also retired,

00:06:50.069 --> 00:06:52.130
and then he just wasn't the same.

00:06:52.130 --> 00:06:53.256
You hear that more often,

00:06:53.256 --> 00:06:54.273
so I look into this

00:06:54.273 --> 00:06:55.106
concept of retirement.

00:06:55.106 --> 00:06:56.069
Here's two things I find out

00:06:56.069 --> 00:06:56.977
that are interesting.

00:06:56.977 --> 00:06:58.405
Number one, Fortune Magazine says

00:06:58.405 --> 00:06:59.636
the two most dangerous years

00:06:59.636 --> 00:07:01.642
of your life physically are

00:07:01.642 --> 00:07:03.555
the year you were born.

00:07:03.555 --> 00:07:04.388
Infant mortality over

00:07:04.388 --> 00:07:05.267
the entire lifespan of

00:07:05.267 --> 00:07:07.316
the human race is 40 percent, okay?

00:07:07.316 --> 00:07:08.835
That's kind of a big number.

00:07:08.835 --> 00:07:11.050
Better now, but 40 percent.

00:07:11.050 --> 00:07:14.758
Number two, the year you retire, okay?

00:07:14.758 --> 00:07:15.873
Big deal.

00:07:15.873 --> 00:07:17.010
The New York Times reports that

00:07:17.010 --> 00:07:19.452
the incidences of depression spike

00:07:19.452 --> 00:07:22.275
40 percent in that year you retire,

00:07:22.275 --> 00:07:23.471
so I started looking around the world

00:07:23.471 --> 00:07:24.885
at this concept of work,

00:07:24.885 --> 00:07:26.239
and of leaving work,

00:07:26.239 --> 00:07:27.415
and ceasing to work.

00:07:27.415 --> 00:07:28.531
Here's what I find.

00:07:28.531 --> 00:07:29.364
Dan Buettner,

00:07:29.364 --> 00:07:30.378
and National Geographic did

00:07:30.378 --> 00:07:31.821
study on the Blue Zones.

00:07:31.821 --> 00:07:32.937
Some of you may have heard of it.

00:07:32.937 --> 00:07:33.948
It's the places around the world

00:07:33.948 --> 00:07:34.954
where people live like

00:07:34.954 --> 00:07:36.663
way longer than we do.

00:07:36.663 --> 00:07:37.771
He studied a place called

00:07:37.771 --> 00:07:39.786
Okinawa in Japan where they live

00:07:39.786 --> 00:07:41.220
on average seven years longer

00:07:41.220 --> 00:07:42.731
than in North America,

00:07:42.731 --> 00:07:45.161
and guess what they call retirement?

00:07:45.161 --> 00:07:46.508
Nothing, they don't even have

00:07:46.508 --> 00:07:48.044
a word for retirement.

00:07:48.044 --> 00:07:49.694
Literally, nothing in their language

00:07:49.694 --> 00:07:53.073
describes the concept of stopping work.

00:07:53.073 --> 00:07:55.339
Instead they have a word which I love

00:07:55.339 --> 00:07:57.589
called Ikigai, I-k-i-g-a-i,

00:07:59.267 --> 00:08:01.197
Ikigai which roughly translates as

00:08:01.197 --> 00:08:02.813
the reason you get out of bed

00:08:02.813 --> 00:08:04.545
in the morning, okay?

00:08:04.545 --> 00:08:06.034
What is your Ikigai?

00:08:06.034 --> 00:08:07.552
So I gave my wife two little cards

00:08:07.552 --> 00:08:08.669
at Christmas last year.

00:08:08.669 --> 00:08:09.748
We put them on our bedside tables.

00:08:09.748 --> 00:08:10.965
She's an elementary school

00:08:10.965 --> 00:08:11.991
teacher at the TDSB.

00:08:11.991 --> 00:08:14.179
She writes to educate leaders

00:08:14.179 --> 00:08:16.147
who make a difference in the world.

00:08:16.147 --> 00:08:18.195
I write finish writing this book,

00:08:18.195 --> 00:08:19.377
or, you know, to increase

00:08:19.377 --> 00:08:20.532
happiness inside organizations,

00:08:20.532 --> 00:08:21.635
or whatever I'm focusing on,

00:08:21.635 --> 00:08:22.905
but it gives you a bit of direction.

00:08:22.905 --> 00:08:24.505
It gives you a direction,

00:08:24.505 --> 00:08:26.666
so I advise and I recommend

00:08:26.666 --> 00:08:28.272
you never retire plus

00:08:28.272 --> 00:08:29.676
just to throw in there

00:08:29.676 --> 00:08:32.302
retirement was invented, okay?

00:08:32.302 --> 00:08:33.523
It wasn't like we used to do this.

00:08:33.523 --> 00:08:36.168
We invented it in 1889 in Germany

00:08:36.168 --> 00:08:37.731
when Chancellor Otto von Bismarck

00:08:37.731 --> 00:08:39.176
just arbitrarily said,

00:08:39.176 --> 00:08:41.509
hey guys, retirement age 65,

00:08:43.019 --> 00:08:44.769
and life span was 67.

00:08:47.406 --> 00:08:49.828
Now we all want to retire earlier,

00:08:49.828 --> 00:08:52.432
and we live way longer,

00:08:52.432 --> 00:08:53.599
so those years

00:08:54.799 --> 00:08:56.277
can be vacuous,

00:08:56.277 --> 00:08:58.340
and they can be troublesome

00:08:58.340 --> 00:08:59.532
for all the reasons I mentioned,

00:08:59.532 --> 00:09:01.922
so I advise people to have an Ikigai,

00:09:01.922 --> 00:09:03.413
and to find work that

00:09:03.413 --> 00:09:04.800
includes the four S's.

00:09:04.800 --> 00:09:06.014
Social fulfillment,

00:09:06.014 --> 00:09:06.847
the structure of getting

00:09:06.847 --> 00:09:08.619
out of bed in the morning.

00:09:08.619 --> 00:09:09.609
The stimulation of always

00:09:09.609 --> 00:09:10.549
learning new things,

00:09:10.549 --> 00:09:12.003
and the story of being part of

00:09:12.003 --> 00:09:13.280
something bigger than yourself,

00:09:13.280 --> 00:09:14.663
something you couldn't do by yourself.

00:09:14.663 --> 00:09:15.777
If you have those four S's

00:09:15.777 --> 00:09:16.742
then you have what I call

00:09:16.742 --> 00:09:18.396
meaningful work.

00:09:18.396 --> 00:09:19.239
- If you're not doing

00:09:19.239 --> 00:09:20.869
the work that you love

00:09:20.869 --> 00:09:22.796
how can you tap into that

00:09:22.796 --> 00:09:23.862
sense of purpose,

00:09:23.862 --> 00:09:25.939
and meaning, and joy?

00:09:25.939 --> 00:09:27.232
- Great question,

00:09:27.232 --> 00:09:28.088
and I'm not advocating that

00:09:28.088 --> 00:09:29.427
someone about to punch out of

00:09:29.427 --> 00:09:31.023
30 years in the meat packing plant

00:09:31.023 --> 00:09:32.380
sort of punch in for 30 more years.

00:09:32.380 --> 00:09:33.234
I'm not saying that.

00:09:33.234 --> 00:09:35.757
What I am saying is that we all have

00:09:35.757 --> 00:09:38.578
168 hours in a week, okay?

00:09:38.578 --> 00:09:39.411
You have that many,

00:09:39.411 --> 00:09:40.244
I have that many,

00:09:40.244 --> 00:09:41.454
Justin Trudeau has that many,

00:09:41.454 --> 00:09:43.309
Mark Zuckerberg has that many,

00:09:43.309 --> 00:09:44.738
Warren Buffet has that many.

00:09:44.738 --> 00:09:45.571
The richest men in the world

00:09:45.571 --> 00:09:46.452
can't buy more time.

00:09:46.452 --> 00:09:47.712
It's the one thing that's not for sale,

00:09:47.712 --> 00:09:50.323
so if you take 168 hours in a week,

00:09:50.323 --> 00:09:51.842
and you divide it by three

00:09:51.842 --> 00:09:53.826
you get three buckets of 56.

00:09:53.826 --> 00:09:55.076
56, 56, and 56.

00:09:56.088 --> 00:09:57.203
If you are lucky enough

00:09:57.203 --> 00:09:58.036
to sleep eight hours a night,

00:09:58.036 --> 00:09:59.760
and with two small children I am not,

00:09:59.760 --> 00:10:01.492
but if you are then

00:10:01.492 --> 00:10:03.369
that's 56 hours a week, okay?

00:10:03.369 --> 00:10:04.786
If you work 56 hours a week

00:10:04.786 --> 00:10:07.812
as whatever job you don't like, okay?

00:10:07.812 --> 00:10:09.009
That might be your middle bucket,

00:10:09.009 --> 00:10:10.328
however, I would still argue that

00:10:10.328 --> 00:10:12.133
those two buckets justify,

00:10:12.133 --> 00:10:14.850
create, and pay for your third bucket

00:10:14.850 --> 00:10:17.684
the whatever you want to do bucket.

00:10:17.684 --> 00:10:18.818
Is that where you volunteer?

00:10:18.818 --> 00:10:19.651
Is that where you're

00:10:19.651 --> 00:10:20.484
in the church group?

00:10:20.484 --> 00:10:21.317
Is that where you're doing

00:10:21.317 --> 00:10:22.724
something you love?

00:10:22.724 --> 00:10:23.831
Even when the meat packing plant

00:10:23.831 --> 00:10:25.636
is over, or the gig is done

00:10:25.636 --> 00:10:27.911
then your bucket can expand,

00:10:27.911 --> 00:10:29.425
and you could keep doing work.

00:10:29.425 --> 00:10:31.088
The definition of retirement I'm using

00:10:31.088 --> 00:10:33.216
is ceasing work completely,

00:10:33.216 --> 00:10:34.718
becoming idle, okay?

00:10:34.718 --> 00:10:35.551
- Yeah.

00:10:35.551 --> 00:10:36.703
- Many people who are like on retirement

00:10:36.703 --> 00:10:37.974
they're like I love retirement.

00:10:37.974 --> 00:10:39.050
I volunteer at the hospital.

00:10:39.050 --> 00:10:40.716
I'm always at the library, and I do...

00:10:40.716 --> 00:10:41.667
You're not retired.

00:10:41.667 --> 00:10:43.434
You're doing 100 things, okay?

00:10:43.434 --> 00:10:45.240
So that's what I'm saying.

00:10:45.240 --> 00:10:47.104
It's like keep finding things you love.

00:10:47.104 --> 00:10:49.205
How do you handle setbacks?

00:10:49.205 --> 00:10:50.212
That's a great question,

00:10:50.212 --> 00:10:51.045
and, sometimes, people say like

00:10:51.045 --> 00:10:51.878
do you have setbacks?

00:10:51.878 --> 00:10:53.165
Like, yeah, lots, I mentioned,

00:10:53.165 --> 00:10:54.075
I kind of joked at the beginning

00:10:54.075 --> 00:10:54.908
I'm like there's tons

00:10:54.908 --> 00:10:56.283
of failures in there.

00:10:56.283 --> 00:10:57.387
The thing is, you know,

00:10:57.387 --> 00:10:58.656
if you ask a good photographer

00:10:58.656 --> 00:10:59.979
how do you take such good pictures?

00:10:59.979 --> 00:11:01.195
Do you know what most of them say?

00:11:01.195 --> 00:11:02.612
I just take lots.

00:11:03.528 --> 00:11:05.024
You've heard this before.

00:11:05.024 --> 00:11:06.848
It's like I just take thousands,

00:11:06.848 --> 00:11:08.849
so that's why I always get 10 great ones

00:11:08.849 --> 00:11:10.772
because I take so many more,

00:11:10.772 --> 00:11:13.389
and like I think it's like a table

00:11:13.389 --> 00:11:14.825
with legs on it.

00:11:14.825 --> 00:11:16.579
That's your life.

00:11:16.579 --> 00:11:17.743
If you have a table like this

00:11:17.743 --> 00:11:18.721
with one leg on it,

00:11:18.721 --> 00:11:19.554
and I know because I was here before,

00:11:19.554 --> 00:11:20.579
and I knocked it over.

00:11:20.579 --> 00:11:22.180
It can fall over.

00:11:22.180 --> 00:11:23.615
It can fall over pretty easily.

00:11:23.615 --> 00:11:25.209
I call that one table.

00:11:25.209 --> 00:11:26.042
You can think of it in terms of

00:11:26.042 --> 00:11:26.875
it's up to you.

00:11:26.875 --> 00:11:29.168
Cashflow streams if you want.

00:11:29.168 --> 00:11:31.134
Relationships if you want,

00:11:31.134 --> 00:11:32.188
but for me it's like

00:11:32.188 --> 00:11:33.642
how many legs does your table have?

00:11:33.642 --> 00:11:34.682
So how do I handle setbacks?

00:11:34.682 --> 00:11:35.751
I try to have a multi-legged

00:11:35.751 --> 00:11:37.247
table at all times.

00:11:37.247 --> 00:11:38.491
Like I said, at Walmart

00:11:38.491 --> 00:11:39.784
I was doing my blogs on my books.

00:11:39.784 --> 00:11:40.845
It made me more risk-taking

00:11:40.845 --> 00:11:42.207
in my writing to know

00:11:42.207 --> 00:11:43.162
I had a full-time job.

00:11:43.162 --> 00:11:44.988
It made me more risk-taking in meetings.

00:11:44.988 --> 00:11:45.944
I was mouthy and asking

00:11:45.944 --> 00:11:46.938
questions of the CEO

00:11:46.938 --> 00:11:48.046
'cause I'm like, yeah, fire me,

00:11:48.046 --> 00:11:49.053
I got this other thing.

00:11:49.053 --> 00:11:49.907
You know what I mean?

00:11:49.907 --> 00:11:51.407
That's two legs on my table.

00:11:51.407 --> 00:11:52.557
Two legs right there,

00:11:52.557 --> 00:11:54.454
and then if I add a third thing,

00:11:54.454 --> 00:11:55.494
a passive income stream,

00:11:55.494 --> 00:11:56.327
or I'm doing something online,

00:11:56.327 --> 00:11:57.423
or I'm speaking now, whatever,

00:11:57.423 --> 00:11:58.748
it's multi-legged.

00:11:58.748 --> 00:12:00.365
How do I handle setbacks?

00:12:00.365 --> 00:12:02.081
I look at the other legs.

00:12:02.081 --> 00:12:03.466
I think about what else I got going on,

00:12:03.466 --> 00:12:04.773
let that thing naturally come back,

00:12:04.773 --> 00:12:05.893
it's okay.

00:12:05.893 --> 00:12:07.402
Just be doing multiple things at once.

00:12:07.402 --> 00:12:09.033
To me that's how I think about it.

00:12:09.033 --> 00:12:10.700
It never makes a low feel super low

00:12:10.700 --> 00:12:13.275
because I'm not all in on anything.

00:12:13.275 --> 00:12:15.419
The secret to turning your biggest fear

00:12:15.419 --> 00:12:17.812
into your biggest success.

00:12:17.812 --> 00:12:19.024
I'm embarrassed to admit this,

00:12:19.024 --> 00:12:20.145
especially, because we're filming this,

00:12:20.145 --> 00:12:22.257
so it's going to be on YouTube forever,

00:12:22.257 --> 00:12:23.145
but I'll say it right now

00:12:23.145 --> 00:12:25.179
for the first time.

00:12:25.179 --> 00:12:27.346
I didn't know how to swim

00:12:28.269 --> 00:12:31.346
at all in my early 30's, okay?

00:12:31.346 --> 00:12:32.193
I'm 36 now,

00:12:32.193 --> 00:12:34.451
and why didn't I know how to swim?

00:12:34.451 --> 00:12:35.829
My sister could swim, and everything.

00:12:35.829 --> 00:12:37.784
It's because I had ear infections

00:12:37.784 --> 00:12:38.978
my whole childhood,

00:12:38.978 --> 00:12:40.605
so I had tubes in my ears

00:12:40.605 --> 00:12:41.703
my whole childhood,

00:12:41.703 --> 00:12:42.656
and I had a traumatic experience

00:12:42.656 --> 00:12:43.932
on top of all that

00:12:43.932 --> 00:12:44.959
when I fell into the pool,

00:12:44.959 --> 00:12:47.313
and I couldn't swim.

00:12:47.313 --> 00:12:49.980
I can almost still see it today,

00:12:50.821 --> 00:12:51.945
so that's fine

00:12:51.945 --> 00:12:52.968
because you know what?

00:12:52.968 --> 00:12:54.616
Who cares I don't need to swim.

00:12:54.616 --> 00:12:55.600
I live in Toronto.

00:12:55.600 --> 00:12:57.973
There's no ocean, I'm good.

00:12:57.973 --> 00:12:58.806
I wouldn't even bring my

00:12:58.806 --> 00:13:00.345
bathing suit to the pool party.

00:13:00.345 --> 00:13:01.178
My friends would want to

00:13:01.178 --> 00:13:02.117
go swimming at university

00:13:02.117 --> 00:13:04.087
I hang out with the treadmill,

00:13:04.087 --> 00:13:05.281
no problem.

00:13:05.281 --> 00:13:08.166
I eliminated swimming from my life.

00:13:08.166 --> 00:13:10.030
You see, I didn't think I can do it.

00:13:10.030 --> 00:13:10.863
I didn't think I had

00:13:10.863 --> 00:13:12.085
the capability to do it,

00:13:12.085 --> 00:13:14.382
and I certainly didn't want to do it,

00:13:14.382 --> 00:13:17.198
you know, I had no motivation,

00:13:17.198 --> 00:13:19.188
so I never did it,

00:13:19.188 --> 00:13:20.021
and that's how we think

00:13:20.021 --> 00:13:21.506
about anything, right?

00:13:21.506 --> 00:13:22.858
You got to have the capability,

00:13:22.858 --> 00:13:24.279
then the motivation,

00:13:24.279 --> 00:13:27.140
and then you have the action.

00:13:27.140 --> 00:13:29.252
I never got to do because

00:13:29.252 --> 00:13:31.866
these things were never going to happen.

00:13:31.866 --> 00:13:32.699
All that changed on

00:13:32.699 --> 00:13:34.604
my second date with Leslie

00:13:34.604 --> 00:13:35.437
when she said to me

00:13:35.437 --> 00:13:36.948
over dinner one night.

00:13:36.948 --> 00:13:39.198
"So, do you like swimming?"

00:13:40.074 --> 00:13:41.523
I was thinking to myself,

00:13:41.523 --> 00:13:43.772
oh-oh, play it cool Pasricha.

00:13:43.772 --> 00:13:46.781
You don't want to scare her off,

00:13:46.781 --> 00:13:49.046
and I said to her, nah.

00:13:49.046 --> 00:13:50.446
Play it cool.

00:13:50.446 --> 00:13:52.327
No, I'm not really a big fan.

00:13:52.327 --> 00:13:53.630
She says, "Ugh,

00:13:53.630 --> 00:13:55.426
"that's too bad.

00:13:55.426 --> 00:13:58.325
"You see, my family's had a cottage

00:13:58.325 --> 00:14:01.448
"on an island for generations,

00:14:01.448 --> 00:14:03.120
"and there's 20 of us up there

00:14:03.120 --> 00:14:04.093
"the whole summer.

00:14:04.093 --> 00:14:05.200
"My 80-year-old grandparents.

00:14:05.200 --> 00:14:06.481
"My five-year-old cousins.

00:14:06.481 --> 00:14:09.503
"We get up every morning in the summer

00:14:09.503 --> 00:14:10.921
"jump into the lake,

00:14:10.921 --> 00:14:12.380
"and swim around the island.

00:14:12.380 --> 00:14:14.295
"It takes about half an hour.

00:14:14.295 --> 00:14:16.795
"Man, I guess you can't come."

00:14:18.896 --> 00:14:20.532
Suddenly, that night

00:14:20.532 --> 00:14:21.365
without thinking about whether

00:14:21.365 --> 00:14:23.213
I can do it, or whether

00:14:23.213 --> 00:14:26.195
I want to do it I just did it.

00:14:26.195 --> 00:14:28.271
I signed up for adults

00:14:28.271 --> 00:14:30.175
learn to swim classes at

00:14:30.175 --> 00:14:32.343
the City of Toronto downtown pool.

00:14:32.343 --> 00:14:33.415
I don't know how many people here

00:14:33.415 --> 00:14:34.372
have seen the downtown

00:14:34.372 --> 00:14:35.205
City of Toronto pool

00:14:35.205 --> 00:14:37.154
this isn't a recommended thing to do.

00:14:37.154 --> 00:14:39.109
It's like, you know, right, you know,

00:14:39.109 --> 00:14:40.833
right in the urban center of the city.

00:14:40.833 --> 00:14:42.546
I'm like getting my life jacket,

00:14:42.546 --> 00:14:44.386
the goggles, the whole thing.

00:14:44.386 --> 00:14:45.609
I'm walking onto the pool deck

00:14:45.609 --> 00:14:47.652
that first Wednesday night.

00:14:47.652 --> 00:14:49.338
My heart is beating louder

00:14:49.338 --> 00:14:50.467
than it had in years.

00:14:50.467 --> 00:14:52.055
I was so nervous,

00:14:52.055 --> 00:14:53.028
but you know what happened when

00:14:53.028 --> 00:14:54.306
I walked in on that pool deck, Rachel,

00:14:54.306 --> 00:14:56.136
I get out there, I get out there,

00:14:56.136 --> 00:14:57.526
and you know who's out there?

00:14:57.526 --> 00:14:59.038
A whole bunch of people

00:14:59.038 --> 00:15:00.495
who suck at swimming.

00:15:00.495 --> 00:15:02.366
For once I'm not the only one.

00:15:02.366 --> 00:15:03.840
People are from landlocked countries.

00:15:03.840 --> 00:15:04.688
They had more traumatic

00:15:04.688 --> 00:15:06.608
experiences than me.

00:15:06.608 --> 00:15:09.372
Trust formed very quickly.

00:15:09.372 --> 00:15:10.675
So we get into the shallow end

00:15:10.675 --> 00:15:12.009
with the flutter board,

00:15:12.009 --> 00:15:13.887
the life jacket, the goggles,

00:15:13.887 --> 00:15:15.547
and within five minutes of doing that

00:15:15.547 --> 00:15:16.865
where I could touch the bottom

00:15:16.865 --> 00:15:19.152
I was like, hey wait a minute.

00:15:19.152 --> 00:15:20.792
I can do that.

00:15:20.792 --> 00:15:23.459
Like that's not hard I can do that,

00:15:23.459 --> 00:15:24.656
and then you know what?

00:15:24.656 --> 00:15:27.323
The next week I wanted to do it.

00:15:28.373 --> 00:15:29.206
I wanted to do it

00:15:29.206 --> 00:15:30.074
because I was like, well,

00:15:30.074 --> 00:15:31.760
I kind of thought I can do it now,

00:15:31.760 --> 00:15:33.702
so the next week I was like

00:15:33.702 --> 00:15:35.203
flutter kicking in the deep end.

00:15:35.203 --> 00:15:36.397
The week after flutter kicking

00:15:36.397 --> 00:15:37.605
with no life jacket.

00:15:37.605 --> 00:15:38.585
By the end of the eight weeks

00:15:38.585 --> 00:15:40.918
I was doing the front crawl.

00:15:42.645 --> 00:15:44.978
The thing I realized is that

00:15:45.839 --> 00:15:47.223
motivation doesn't

00:15:47.223 --> 00:15:49.171
actually lead to action

00:15:49.171 --> 00:15:50.671
like we all think.

00:15:51.684 --> 00:15:53.934
Action leads to motivation.

00:15:55.358 --> 00:15:57.358
It's completely flipped.

00:15:58.597 --> 00:16:00.795
You want to write that novel.

00:16:00.795 --> 00:16:02.568
You don't need the perfect coffee shop,

00:16:02.568 --> 00:16:03.498
the right idea,

00:16:03.498 --> 00:16:05.243
and the expensive moleskin.

00:16:05.243 --> 00:16:07.309
Just write one sentence with

00:16:07.309 --> 00:16:09.732
a pen from the hotel on a Post-It note.

00:16:09.732 --> 00:16:11.665
Just doing that will tell you

00:16:11.665 --> 00:16:12.828
that you can do it,

00:16:12.828 --> 00:16:14.309
and then you'll want to do it.

00:16:14.309 --> 00:16:15.383
You want to run a marathon?

00:16:15.383 --> 00:16:16.377
Forget the good shoes,

00:16:16.377 --> 00:16:17.782
the right place, and the running buddy.

00:16:17.782 --> 00:16:18.921
Forget it.

00:16:18.921 --> 00:16:19.920
Just run to the stop sign

00:16:19.920 --> 00:16:21.547
in your dress shoes.

00:16:21.547 --> 00:16:22.553
By the time you do that

00:16:22.553 --> 00:16:24.238
you're like I can do that,

00:16:24.238 --> 00:16:26.279
and then you think you can do it,

00:16:26.279 --> 00:16:28.015
and then you'll want to do it.

00:16:28.015 --> 00:16:30.390
It's the exact opposite.

00:16:30.390 --> 00:16:31.952
It's the exact opposite.

00:16:31.952 --> 00:16:33.081
Don't just take it from me.

00:16:33.081 --> 00:16:34.374
You guys are scientists.

00:16:34.374 --> 00:16:35.207
You probably trust someone

00:16:35.207 --> 00:16:36.689
like Issac Newton, you know.

00:16:36.689 --> 00:16:38.405
He's the greatest physicist

00:16:38.405 --> 00:16:39.473
of all time after all,

00:16:39.473 --> 00:16:40.933
I mean, up there with Einstein.

00:16:40.933 --> 00:16:42.316
The guy discovered gravity,

00:16:42.316 --> 00:16:43.838
invented calculus.

00:16:43.838 --> 00:16:44.994
Pretty good resume.

00:16:44.994 --> 00:16:46.894
He'd probably be hired
at Google these days.

00:16:46.894 --> 00:16:47.866
He actually said

00:16:47.866 --> 00:16:48.819
in his first law of motion like

00:16:48.819 --> 00:16:50.051
I can remember like grade 10 science.

00:16:50.051 --> 00:16:51.634
An object in motion

00:16:52.488 --> 00:16:54.949
will remain in motion

00:16:54.949 --> 00:16:56.597
unless acted upon by

00:16:56.597 --> 00:16:58.774
an equal or greater force.

00:16:58.774 --> 00:16:59.826
Put another way

00:16:59.826 --> 00:17:00.831
if you start doing it

00:17:00.831 --> 00:17:02.960
you place action first you'll continue.

00:17:02.960 --> 00:17:04.430
It's harder to stop,

00:17:04.430 --> 00:17:05.612
and that's why at the end of

00:17:05.612 --> 00:17:06.677
my adult learn to swim classes

00:17:06.677 --> 00:17:07.789
I signed up again,

00:17:07.789 --> 00:17:09.158
and I signed up again,

00:17:09.158 --> 00:17:10.136
and I signed up again,

00:17:10.136 --> 00:17:11.858
and it was the same exact class.

00:17:11.858 --> 00:17:13.402
I didn't go to learn to swim two.

00:17:13.402 --> 00:17:14.907
I just did learn to swim one.

00:17:14.907 --> 00:17:17.990
I'm like I love this, it's amazing.

00:17:17.990 --> 00:17:19.324
I can do the front crawl.

00:17:19.324 --> 00:17:20.886
I loved it so much I just kept

00:17:20.886 --> 00:17:23.612
doing it over and over again.

00:17:23.612 --> 00:17:24.695
It is easier,

00:17:25.601 --> 00:17:26.601
it is easier

00:17:27.583 --> 00:17:29.551
to act yourself into

00:17:29.551 --> 00:17:31.507
a new way of thinking,

00:17:31.507 --> 00:17:32.851
and then to think yourself

00:17:32.851 --> 00:17:34.878
into a new way of acting.

00:17:34.878 --> 00:17:37.240
Our brains are often the enemy.

00:17:37.240 --> 00:17:38.366
How do you do it?

00:17:38.366 --> 00:17:39.521
How do you make your biggest fear

00:17:39.521 --> 00:17:41.339
into your biggest success?

00:17:41.339 --> 00:17:43.096
You just do it.

00:17:43.096 --> 00:17:44.783
You put action first.

00:17:44.783 --> 00:17:46.780
We all know Nike's slogan Just Do It.

00:17:46.780 --> 00:17:48.128
Well, you probably don't know is that

00:17:48.128 --> 00:17:49.502
Nike was an $800 million company

00:17:49.502 --> 00:17:51.986
with low 20 percent market share

00:17:51.986 --> 00:17:53.217
when they came up with this slogan.

00:17:53.217 --> 00:17:55.400
Then they struck a nerve

00:17:55.400 --> 00:17:56.706
deep inside of us

00:17:56.706 --> 00:17:58.448
something we all believe is true.

00:17:58.448 --> 00:17:59.376
You know what?

00:17:59.376 --> 00:18:00.882
On the heels of the slogan

00:18:00.882 --> 00:18:02.543
$10 billion company,

00:18:02.543 --> 00:18:04.583
50, 60 percent market share.

00:18:04.583 --> 00:18:05.849
That's over the lifetime

00:18:05.849 --> 00:18:07.073
of using the slogan.

00:18:07.073 --> 00:18:08.797
We can remember the ad that jazzed you up.

00:18:08.797 --> 00:18:09.724
You're like I can do that

00:18:09.724 --> 00:18:11.215
just get out there and do it,

00:18:11.215 --> 00:18:13.418
and it works, it works.

00:18:13.418 --> 00:18:15.025
Look, we're all going to get lumps,

00:18:15.025 --> 00:18:17.987
and we're all going to get bumps.

00:18:17.987 --> 00:18:18.911
None of us can predict the future,

00:18:18.911 --> 00:18:20.335
but we do know one thing about it,

00:18:20.335 --> 00:18:21.235
and that's that

00:18:21.235 --> 00:18:24.159
it ain't going to go according to plan.

00:18:24.159 --> 00:18:25.304
We will all have high highs,

00:18:25.304 --> 00:18:26.625
and big days, and proud moments of

00:18:26.625 --> 00:18:28.839
smiles on graduation stages,

00:18:28.839 --> 00:18:30.927
father-daughter dances at weddings,

00:18:30.927 --> 00:18:32.478
and healthy babies

00:18:32.478 --> 00:18:34.858
screeching in the delivery room,

00:18:34.858 --> 00:18:35.874
but between those high highs

00:18:35.874 --> 00:18:37.633
we may also have some lumps,

00:18:37.633 --> 00:18:39.650
and some bumps, too.

00:18:39.650 --> 00:18:42.101
It's sad, you know, it's not pleasant

00:18:42.101 --> 00:18:44.215
to talk about, but you know

00:18:44.215 --> 00:18:45.842
your husband might leave you.

00:18:45.842 --> 00:18:47.745
Your girlfriend could cheat.

00:18:47.745 --> 00:18:49.340
Your headaches might be

00:18:49.340 --> 00:18:50.700
more serious than you thought,

00:18:50.700 --> 00:18:52.802
or your dog could get hit by

00:18:52.802 --> 00:18:54.429
a car in the street.

00:18:54.429 --> 00:18:56.433
It's not a happy thought,

00:18:56.433 --> 00:18:57.750
but your kids could get mixed up

00:18:57.750 --> 00:18:59.667
in gangs or bad scenes.

00:19:01.634 --> 00:19:04.179
Your mom could get cancer.

00:19:04.179 --> 00:19:05.959
Your dad could get mean.

00:19:05.959 --> 00:19:07.349
There are times in life when

00:19:07.349 --> 00:19:08.884
you will be tossed on the well, too,

00:19:08.884 --> 00:19:10.032
with twists in your stomach,

00:19:10.032 --> 00:19:11.156
and with holes in your heart,

00:19:11.156 --> 00:19:13.094
and when that bad news washes over you,

00:19:13.094 --> 00:19:14.452
and when that pain sponges,

00:19:14.452 --> 00:19:17.192
and soaks in I just really hope you feel

00:19:17.192 --> 00:19:20.063
that you've always got two choices.

00:19:20.063 --> 00:19:22.175
One, you can swirl and twirl

00:19:22.175 --> 00:19:24.792
in gloom and doom forever,

00:19:24.792 --> 00:19:26.824
or two, you can grieve,

00:19:26.824 --> 00:19:28.184
and then face the future

00:19:28.184 --> 00:19:30.360
with newly sober eyes.

00:19:30.360 --> 00:19:31.732
Having a great attitude is

00:19:31.732 --> 00:19:33.480
about choosing option number two,

00:19:33.480 --> 00:19:34.313
and choosing,

00:19:34.313 --> 00:19:35.393
no matter how difficult it is,

00:19:35.393 --> 00:19:36.632
no matter what pain hits you

00:19:36.632 --> 00:19:38.908
choosing to move forward, and move on,

00:19:38.908 --> 00:19:41.014
and take baby steps into the future.

00:19:41.014 --> 00:19:42.146
I have a really strong

00:19:42.146 --> 00:19:43.310
viewpoint on advice,

00:19:43.310 --> 00:19:45.140
and it's a bit counterintuitive,

00:19:45.140 --> 00:19:46.838
and maybe sometimes controversial.

00:19:46.838 --> 00:19:49.890
The thing is there's a famous quote

00:19:49.890 --> 00:19:50.857
from the 1800's that says

00:19:50.857 --> 00:19:52.491
when we are asking for advice

00:19:52.491 --> 00:19:54.713
we're really looking for an accomplice.

00:19:54.713 --> 00:19:56.561
We're almost always looking for

00:19:56.561 --> 00:19:57.923
someone to either agree

00:19:57.923 --> 00:19:58.756
with what we want,

00:19:58.756 --> 00:19:59.787
or disagree with it,

00:19:59.787 --> 00:20:00.785
so we can kind of

00:20:00.785 --> 00:20:02.092
stand firm in our viewpoint.

00:20:02.092 --> 00:20:02.947
You ask people what you

00:20:02.947 --> 00:20:03.780
should name their kid,

00:20:03.780 --> 00:20:04.627
or what school you should go to.

00:20:04.627 --> 00:20:05.460
You might already have

00:20:05.460 --> 00:20:07.679
an idea in your mind.

00:20:07.679 --> 00:20:08.512
One day I was surfing

00:20:08.512 --> 00:20:09.345
through the newspaper.

00:20:09.345 --> 00:20:10.178
I noticed the cover of

00:20:10.178 --> 00:20:11.011
The New York Times,

00:20:11.011 --> 00:20:12.103
and the cover of The Toronto Star

00:20:12.103 --> 00:20:13.273
the biggest two newspapers

00:20:13.273 --> 00:20:14.650
in both countries had

00:20:14.650 --> 00:20:16.439
the exact opposite advice on them.

00:20:16.439 --> 00:20:20.236
One said no need to get extra Vitamin D

00:20:20.236 --> 00:20:21.380
'cause there's been a study,

00:20:21.380 --> 00:20:23.421
and 1,000 people have been in the study,

00:20:23.421 --> 00:20:24.530
and all the research says

00:20:24.530 --> 00:20:25.683
you don't need more Vitamin D.

00:20:25.683 --> 00:20:26.931
The other newspaper said

00:20:26.931 --> 00:20:28.839
go on the advice to get your Vitamin D.

00:20:28.839 --> 00:20:29.672
None of us are getting enough.

00:20:29.672 --> 00:20:31.172
The earth is tilting away from the sun,

00:20:31.172 --> 00:20:33.598
like, guys, this is a big problem.

00:20:33.598 --> 00:20:35.259
I'm like it's like huge,

00:20:35.259 --> 00:20:36.401
credible news sources

00:20:36.401 --> 00:20:38.012
with studies backing both of them

00:20:38.012 --> 00:20:39.724
on the front page the same day

00:20:39.724 --> 00:20:41.577
showing the exact opposite advice.

00:20:41.577 --> 00:20:42.846
I started thinking about advice

00:20:42.846 --> 00:20:43.940
the concept of advice,

00:20:43.940 --> 00:20:45.009
and started realizing that

00:20:45.009 --> 00:20:46.558
the most common advice of all

00:20:46.558 --> 00:20:47.756
is actually cliches.

00:20:47.756 --> 00:20:48.589
- Right.

00:20:48.589 --> 00:20:49.422
- So true that you just

00:20:49.422 --> 00:20:50.255
have them memorized,

00:20:50.255 --> 00:20:51.088
and it's like, you know,

00:20:51.088 --> 00:20:53.682
the early bird gets the worm,

00:20:53.682 --> 00:20:54.765
but when you start thinking about it

00:20:54.765 --> 00:20:56.652
you're like or is it good things

00:20:56.652 --> 00:20:59.310
come to those who wait, you know,

00:20:59.310 --> 00:21:00.810
and you start thinking, okay, well,

00:21:00.810 --> 00:21:03.939
it's actions speak louder than words,

00:21:03.939 --> 00:21:05.387
or is it the pen is

00:21:05.387 --> 00:21:07.362
mightier than the sword.

00:21:07.362 --> 00:21:09.227
For every single cliche,

00:21:09.227 --> 00:21:10.528
or piece of advice you ever heard

00:21:10.528 --> 00:21:12.174
there is an equal opposing

00:21:12.174 --> 00:21:13.343
pice of advice,

00:21:13.343 --> 00:21:14.655
so the biggest advice

00:21:14.655 --> 00:21:15.727
I'll leave anybody with

00:21:15.727 --> 00:21:17.200
don't take advice.

00:21:17.200 --> 00:21:18.587
The answers are all inside you.

00:21:18.587 --> 00:21:20.004
You already know what to do,

00:21:20.004 --> 00:21:21.023
and anything you're seeking

00:21:21.023 --> 00:21:22.313
is just trying to confirm something

00:21:22.313 --> 00:21:23.456
you already believe.

00:21:23.456 --> 00:21:25.023
Forget it, you don't need the books,

00:21:25.023 --> 00:21:26.056
you don't need the newspapers,

00:21:26.056 --> 00:21:27.256
you don't need any advice,

00:21:27.256 --> 00:21:28.759
you already know what to do

00:21:28.759 --> 00:21:30.600
just do that.

00:21:30.600 --> 00:21:33.073
I'm 36 now, when I was 20 years old

00:21:33.073 --> 00:21:34.963
I was going to Queen's University.

00:21:34.963 --> 00:21:37.169
Anyone went to Queens here in this room?

00:21:37.169 --> 00:21:38.994
Oh, a lot, good Queens turnout today.

00:21:38.994 --> 00:21:40.077
This is good.

00:21:43.145 --> 00:21:43.978
I was writing for the campus

00:21:43.978 --> 00:21:45.173
humor newspaper Golden Words.

00:21:45.173 --> 00:21:46.659
It was free, like it wasn't

00:21:46.659 --> 00:21:48.423
a paid job, but I loved doing it,

00:21:48.423 --> 00:21:49.256
and I loved writing this

00:21:49.256 --> 00:21:50.761
comedy writing stuff,

00:21:50.761 --> 00:21:53.250
so I took a little paid internship

00:21:53.250 --> 00:21:54.119
down in New York City

00:21:54.119 --> 00:21:55.316
writing for a comedy start-up.

00:21:55.316 --> 00:21:56.550
Simpson's writer,

00:21:56.550 --> 00:21:57.512
Saturday Night Live writers

00:21:57.512 --> 00:21:58.633
living in the Lower East Side.

00:21:58.633 --> 00:21:59.795
I was like this is my dream,

00:21:59.795 --> 00:22:01.134
but it turned into

00:22:01.134 --> 00:22:03.287
the worst job I'd ever had, why?

00:22:03.287 --> 00:22:05.216
Because once I got paid to do

00:22:05.216 --> 00:22:07.162
something I loved naturally,

00:22:07.162 --> 00:22:08.685
suddenly, I had constraints.

00:22:08.685 --> 00:22:09.529
I had to write an article

00:22:09.529 --> 00:22:10.362
about this topic

00:22:10.362 --> 00:22:11.844
with this person by this time,

00:22:11.844 --> 00:22:14.014
and it became so debilitative,

00:22:14.014 --> 00:22:15.893
like limiting and I said to myself

00:22:15.893 --> 00:22:17.656
when I started 1000AwesomeThings.com

00:22:17.656 --> 00:22:19.364
I will never do it for money.

00:22:19.364 --> 00:22:21.697
I never put an ad on the website ever.

00:22:21.697 --> 00:22:24.050
50 million hits it's only cost me money

00:22:24.050 --> 00:22:25.491
through URL renewing,

00:22:25.491 --> 00:22:26.399
you know what I mean?

00:22:26.399 --> 00:22:27.857
It's never made a cent,

00:22:27.857 --> 00:22:29.541
so I was smart about that.

00:22:29.541 --> 00:22:30.965
I was smart about focusing

00:22:30.965 --> 00:22:31.925
the intrinsic motivator of

00:22:31.925 --> 00:22:32.807
doing something you love,

00:22:32.807 --> 00:22:34.481
but I was really dumb about

00:22:34.481 --> 00:22:37.075
starting to focus on stat counters,

00:22:37.075 --> 00:22:39.372
best seller lists,

00:22:39.372 --> 00:22:40.537
those extrinsic motivators

00:22:40.537 --> 00:22:41.658
kept creeping in,

00:22:41.658 --> 00:22:42.945
and you know what the problem is, Rachel?

00:22:42.945 --> 00:22:45.172
They creep into our lives all the time.

00:22:45.172 --> 00:22:46.005
- Right.

00:22:46.005 --> 00:22:46.903
- You can get a job for

00:22:46.903 --> 00:22:47.900
a place you love, cool.

00:22:47.900 --> 00:22:49.063
I'm working for a company I believe in.

00:22:49.063 --> 00:22:50.575
Pretty soon you have a job evaluation.

00:22:50.575 --> 00:22:51.836
You have a salary.

00:22:51.836 --> 00:22:53.151
These are extrinsic motivators,

00:22:53.151 --> 00:22:54.581
and you start targeting your behavior

00:22:54.581 --> 00:22:55.755
to hitting like

00:22:55.755 --> 00:22:57.478
I want to be a solid performer.

00:22:57.478 --> 00:22:58.903
That lowers your performance

00:22:58.903 --> 00:22:59.986
because you're like

00:22:59.986 --> 00:23:00.819
I just need to get somewhere

00:23:00.819 --> 00:23:01.652
in the middle,

00:23:01.652 --> 00:23:02.896
and I get my crappy two percent raise,

00:23:02.896 --> 00:23:05.037
like I don't really get much from that,

00:23:05.037 --> 00:23:07.082
and if they're going to reward me on that

00:23:07.082 --> 00:23:08.334
then the studies all show that

00:23:08.334 --> 00:23:10.351
those extrinsic motivators

00:23:10.351 --> 00:23:12.053
actually block you from seeing

00:23:12.053 --> 00:23:13.264
your intrinsic motivators,

00:23:13.264 --> 00:23:14.602
so Dr. Teresa Amabile

00:23:14.602 --> 00:23:15.863
at Brandeis University

00:23:15.863 --> 00:23:18.149
showed that if you have girls

00:23:18.149 --> 00:23:19.862
teach piano to other girls,

00:23:19.862 --> 00:23:21.676
and you say do it because you love it,

00:23:21.676 --> 00:23:22.704
and you want to teach them piano

00:23:22.704 --> 00:23:23.919
they do an awesome job.

00:23:23.919 --> 00:23:25.022
If you say we'll give you

00:23:25.022 --> 00:23:26.199
a movie ticket if you teach piano

00:23:26.199 --> 00:23:28.154
for half an hour they stink at it.

00:23:28.154 --> 00:23:29.378
They get frustrated.

00:23:29.378 --> 00:23:30.836
They leave after half an hour

00:23:30.836 --> 00:23:32.605
like they're just not very good,

00:23:32.605 --> 00:23:35.059
so it could be that paying,

00:23:35.059 --> 00:23:37.526
or communicating a numerical value

00:23:37.526 --> 00:23:39.114
for something actually reduces

00:23:39.114 --> 00:23:40.540
your own internal motivation,

00:23:40.540 --> 00:23:43.015
so when The Book of Awesome took off

00:23:43.015 --> 00:23:44.356
I got hooked on all that stuff

00:23:44.356 --> 00:23:45.189
because it's so visible,

00:23:45.189 --> 00:23:46.754
so tangible, so real,

00:23:46.754 --> 00:23:48.055
and to answer that question

00:23:48.055 --> 00:23:50.084
for myself with The Happiness Equation

00:23:50.084 --> 00:23:51.646
I drew a new model called

00:23:51.646 --> 00:23:53.134
The Success Triangle.

00:23:53.134 --> 00:23:55.064
I say there's three kinds of success.

00:23:55.064 --> 00:23:56.534
There is sales success.

00:23:56.534 --> 00:23:57.917
There is social success,

00:23:57.917 --> 00:23:59.312
and there is self-success.

00:23:59.312 --> 00:24:00.180
Sales success.

00:24:00.180 --> 00:24:01.436
How many books did you ship?

00:24:01.436 --> 00:24:03.431
Like how many products did you sell?

00:24:03.431 --> 00:24:04.851
How much money did you

00:24:04.851 --> 00:24:05.931
make from something?

00:24:05.931 --> 00:24:07.033
Social success.

00:24:07.033 --> 00:24:08.181
What do other people think?

00:24:08.181 --> 00:24:09.191
What's the critical review?

00:24:09.191 --> 00:24:10.283
Is it nominated for an award?

00:24:10.283 --> 00:24:11.808
Is there a prize associated with it?

00:24:11.808 --> 00:24:13.008
If it's a movie does it get

00:24:13.008 --> 00:24:13.841
an Academy Award?

00:24:13.841 --> 00:24:14.713
Then self-success

00:24:14.713 --> 00:24:16.886
how do you feel about it?

00:24:16.886 --> 00:24:19.046
They can contradict,

00:24:19.046 --> 00:24:20.713
they contradict like

00:24:22.503 --> 00:24:24.170
Hotel Transylvania 2

00:24:25.037 --> 00:24:26.893
won a ton of sales, right?

00:24:26.893 --> 00:24:28.939
And zero Academy Award nominations.

00:24:28.939 --> 00:24:30.914
Spotlight which won best picture

00:24:30.914 --> 00:24:33.236
had $19 million at the box office

00:24:33.236 --> 00:24:34.615
compared to over $200 million

00:24:34.615 --> 00:24:35.741
for Hotel Transylvania 2.

00:24:35.741 --> 00:24:36.574
Why do I know those numbers?

00:24:36.574 --> 00:24:38.051
Because they contradict all the time,

00:24:38.051 --> 00:24:39.641
so the only way to solve it

00:24:39.641 --> 00:24:40.522
is to answer it for yourself,

00:24:40.522 --> 00:24:42.719
and focus on self-success like

00:24:42.719 --> 00:24:44.691
I wrote a letter to my unborn child.

00:24:44.691 --> 00:24:46.277
I hope he gets to read it one day,

00:24:46.277 --> 00:24:47.744
and if I can try my best

00:24:47.744 --> 00:24:49.131
to keep focusing on that mantra

00:24:49.131 --> 00:24:51.373
then it absolves me from worrying about,

00:24:51.373 --> 00:24:52.950
or at least stressing over

00:24:52.950 --> 00:24:54.119
things I can't control.

00:24:54.119 --> 00:24:58.064
I love hanging out with three year olds.

00:24:58.064 --> 00:24:59.667
I love the way that they see the world

00:24:59.667 --> 00:25:00.559
because they're seeing the world

00:25:00.559 --> 00:25:01.837
for the first time.

00:25:01.837 --> 00:25:03.175
I love the way that they can stare

00:25:03.175 --> 00:25:04.890
at a bug crossing the sidewalk.

00:25:04.890 --> 00:25:06.369
I love the way that they'll stare

00:25:06.369 --> 00:25:07.959
slack-jawed at their first baseball game

00:25:07.959 --> 00:25:09.715
with wide eyes and a mitt on their hand

00:25:09.715 --> 00:25:11.462
soaking in the crack of the bat,

00:25:11.462 --> 00:25:12.677
and the crunch of the peanuts,

00:25:12.677 --> 00:25:13.677
and the smell of the hot dogs.

00:25:13.677 --> 00:25:15.293
I love the way that they'll spend hours

00:25:15.293 --> 00:25:17.142
picking dandelions in their backyard,

00:25:17.142 --> 00:25:18.941
and putting them into a nice

00:25:18.941 --> 00:25:22.020
centerpiece for Thanksgiving dinner.

00:25:22.020 --> 00:25:23.499
I love the way that they see the world

00:25:23.499 --> 00:25:24.470
because they're seeing

00:25:24.470 --> 00:25:26.887
the world for the first time.

00:25:29.389 --> 00:25:30.222
Having a sense of awareness

00:25:30.222 --> 00:25:31.567
is just about embracing

00:25:31.567 --> 00:25:33.448
your inner three-year-old

00:25:33.448 --> 00:25:34.282
because you all used to be

00:25:34.282 --> 00:25:35.670
three years old.

00:25:35.670 --> 00:25:36.542
That three-year-old boy

